**Inquiry checkpoint — causal and observational reasoning, 1 November 2026.**

Historical evidence recorded before the skeleton migration. Counts, pending statuses, temporary handles and next-step instructions describe that checkpoint; consult this entrance’s current scope and next steps before acting.

Installed nine new lessons after `@inquiry-learning-review`: `inquiry-causal-question`, `inquiry-causal-identification`, `inquiry-causal-standardization`, `inquiry-causal-weighting`, `inquiry-causal-ambiguity`, `inquiry-causal-adjustment`, `inquiry-causal-missing`, `inquiry-causal-study`, `inquiry-causal-review`. Inquiry now has **82 common learning Records**. Earlier potential-outcome/randomization, conditioning, uncertainty, fitting, reporting and assessment lessons are reused through exact references without copying or rewriting them. No existing shared Record gained a new edge. Three unchanged programs provide exact means, input validation and weighted covariance.

The block distinguishes identification from estimation and defines intervention, unit, target, outcome, time, consistency, exchangeability, positivity and interference limits. Standardization and inverse weighting are derived with explicit conditional expectations. A separate finite known-probability estimator has an unbiasedness proof that does not require independent assignments; its variance formula does require independence. The lesson explicitly rejects carrying that proof over to random estimated ratios or empirical empty cells without further work.

An original four-type service population has true intervention means 6 and 7, but assignment correlated with workload gives crude means 15/2 and 11/2. Standardization recovers the stipulated +1 effect under the stated model. Two additional original models produce exactly the same four-cell observed law while their intervention effects differ, zero versus 1/2; observed overlap alone does not identify the effect. Finite mediator and common-consequence selection examples show why adjusting every column can change the question or introduce association. Incomplete binary outcomes yield sharp bounds on a completed-data group contrast, explicitly not an automatic causal-effect or confidence interval. Sensitivity, interference/carryover, changed-model work, criticism and revision remain part of the learner investigation.

Added `@inquiry-source-causal` for Miguel A. Hernán and James M. Robins, *Causal Inference: What If*. The current author link was followed rather than using the older January 2025 search result. The inspected PDF is dated **19 August 2026**, has **365 pages** and reserves copyright under a 2020/2026 notice; free download does not supply an open redistribution licence. Inspected front matter and selected actual passages in sections 2.2–2.4, 3.1, 3.3–3.4 and 8.4; Table 2.2 on printed page 17 was also visually checked. Source media and companion code remain external. Larger chapter/exercise assignments are not claimed fully audited.

The bounded published reconstruction aggregates Table 2.2's numerical illustration: unadjusted arm means 3/7 and 7/13, standardized/weighted means 1/2 and 1/2. Independent integer replication checks the weighting result. The text's named entries represent large population groups with sampling variation suppressed. The new lesson explicitly prevents applying its imbalance statements as a theorem about an actual twenty-person randomized sample. This is mathematical source reconstruction, not clinical data analysis, an intervention, medical guidance or experimental reproduction. The service and ambiguity examples are original, not relabeled source observations.

