**Inquiry checkpoint — fitting estimation and model criticism, 1 November 2026.**

Historical evidence recorded before the skeleton migration. Counts, pending statuses, temporary handles and next-step instructions describe that checkpoint; consult this entrance’s current scope and next steps before acting.

The fifth inquiry block is installed after `@inquiry-density-review`. Eight new lessons and two preserved metrology Records form this exact continuation: `inquiry-fit-model`, `met-regression`, `inquiry-linear-estimator`, `inquiry-fit-residuals`, `inquiry-fit-prediction`, `met-regression-inverse`, `inquiry-fit-rivals`, `inquiry-fit-replication`, `inquiry-fitting-study`, `inquiry-fitting-review`. Inquiry now has **53 common learning Records**. Original shared identities, prose, imports, source assignments and metrology/gas/clean-manufacturing routes remain intact. Shared least-squares geometry, rank, metrics, model discrepancy and uncertainty are contextual references without competing common edges. The shared exact `linear_space_study.py` is reused unchanged.

The formation distinguishes algebraic fitting, a random estimator and a physical mean model. With fixed x, affine mean, zero-mean errors and covariance σ²I, scalar observation weights derive unbiased coefficients, their covariance and a bounded linear-unbiased optimality statement. Independence and normality are not silently assumed. The residual projection H_ij=1/n+(x_i−x̄)(x_j−x̄)/Sxx gives Cov(r)=σ²(I−H), Σh_i=2 and E(SSE)=(n−2)σ². Mean discrepancy contributes its residual squared norm. Forward mean, independent future reading and random-ratio inversion have separate uncertainty accounts; replacing known σ by an estimate requires a fresh interval argument.

The original finite model uses x=(−2,−1,0,1,2), true mean 2+3x and independent errors ±1 with equal probabilities. Across 32 states, coefficient means are 2,3, variances 1/5,1/10, covariance zero and E(SSE)=3. The chosen signs (1,−1,1,−1,1) yield intercept 11/5, slope 3, residuals (4,−6,4,−6,4)/5 and SSE=24/5. The centre-mean interval with half-width 3/5 covers 30/32; used for an independent future reading it covers 12/64. Future half-width 8/5 covers 62/64. These declared finite-law guarantees are not 95% or Student-t intervals.

A fixed uncorrected offset 2 preserves residuals but makes that narrow interval miss the intended physical mean in every state. A shared random ±1 error gives 64 joint states and intercept variance 6/5, with slope variance and E(SSE) unchanged. Curvature x²/2 gives expected intercept 3, E(SSE)=13/2 and E(s²)=13/6; the chosen sample gives intercept 16/5 and SSE=123/10. Replicated constructed values at inputs (−1,−1,0,0,1,1), responses (−3,−1,7,9,21,23)/4, give intercept 7/3, slope 3, pure-error SS=3/8, lack-of-fit SS=1/3 and total SS=17/24. The partition is algebraic; no unsupported F-test is inferred.

The two original embedded programs `fitting_study.py` and `fitting_checks.py`, with the unchanged shared solver, passed **7,735 exact checks** through Nix/Python 3.14.8. Independent centred scalar fitting checks 1,152 coordinate/parameter cases; pair contrasts check 729 replicated response sets. Enumeration checks coefficient/residual covariance, intervals, model rivals and invalid input behavior. Extraction of all three programs from installed Records to a separate directory passed the same suite. These are synthetic teaching calculations, not instrument validation or performed experiments.

Fresh primary inspection covered selected NIST/SEMATECH pages 4.1.4.1, 4.4.4 and 4.4.4.6 for parameter linearity, residual criticism and replication. The source Record appends this bounded inspection while preserving its identity, broader assignments and rights qualifications. Displayed pressure/temperature output was inspected; no complete published dataset fit was reconstructed. That reconstruction remains an explicit independent-work assignment. No fresh whole-GUM/Possolo inspection, full handbook audit or source-media bundling is claimed.

