The block begins with observable capabilities and distinguishes exposure, assisted performance, independence, retention, transfer and practical competence. It develops prerequisite diagnosis, a worked demonstration, prediction, supported practice, feedback, independent attempts and delayed revisits. The original two-job, three-slot investigation separates aggregate capacity from a feasible schedule. Four calendars have maxima F=2, D=1, O=0, U=1; capacity-only, always-one, correct and reversed-order rules give contrasting predictions. All single tasks fail to distinguish the four stipulated rules; minimal distinguishing pairs are F/D, F/O, D/O and O/U. These are constructed rule comparisons, not inferred psychological types.

Assessment retains correct-number, valid-witness and valid-bound criteria separately, with four contrasting invented responses. A correct number with an invalid bound remains distinguishable from a justified optimum. The scoring account includes criterion revision, assistance, independent judgment versus consensus, correlation versus agreement and limits of score totals. The transfer task changes two serial jobs into drawing/caption prerequisites feeding assembly; a changed waiting rule then requires a revised conclusion. Practical handover uses harmless paper tokens and makes demonstration, guidance, independent execution and teacher preparation distinct. Actual learner observation, outside review and supervised domain practice remain unperformed unless subsequently documented.

