The running evidence is the unchanged fifth-pass fitting investigation and its unchanged mathematical dependency. New `reporting_study.py` and `reporting_checks.py` render and preserve its results, rather than replacing the fitting method. All 21 response values are explicitly synthetic and dimensionless. The common question concerns offset, curvature and input correction, not physical calibration. The four exact summary rows are: affine (n=5, intercept 11/5, slope 3, SSE 24/5, s²8/5); offset (5,21/5,3,24/5,8/5); curvature (5,16/5,3,123/10,41/10); repeated inputs (6,7/3,3,17/24,17/96). The last partition is pure error 3/8 plus lack of fit 1/3, with degrees of freedom 3 and 1. The report distinguishes residual variance, coefficient variance, mean-response variance and future-response variance; it assigns no automatic confidence level.

A claim/premise/evidence table connects every conclusion to its input and method. The shared-offset counterexample preserves slope and residuals while changing the intercept by 2, defeating the claim that residual agreement establishes absolute accuracy. The proposed independent reference observation is not performed. The constructed author/critic exchange is explicitly a teaching example, not outside peer review. Exact rerunning, mathematical agreement, independent implementation, new observations and robustness are distinct evidential activities.

Revision 0 intentionally transcribes affine-3 as 4 instead of 3, leaving every other row unchanged. It gives intercept 12/5, slope 3, SSE 36/5 and s²12/5. Revision 1 corrects that input against the declared generating formula and regenerates every output while retaining revision 0. The coefficient sensitivities are Δα=δ/5 and Δβ=δx_j/10; ΔSSE=2δr_j+δ²(1−h_jj), with h_jj=1/5+x_j²/10. For the central correction δ=−1, the intercept decreases by 1/5 and SSE by 12/5. This is a warranted correction of a known construction, not replacement of an uncertain observation by a model prediction.

